/*!
    \page qt3dxr-coordinates-and-units.html
    \title Coordinate Systems and Units in \qxr

    \brief This page explains how the XR tracking space, the 3D scene, and
    2D Qt Quick content relate to each other, and what a coordinate value
    means in each of them.

    A \qxr application brings together three coordinate systems: the tracking
    space that the XR runtime reports device poses in, the 3D scene inside the
    \l XrView, and the 2D coordinate system of any Qt Quick content the
    application displays. They use different units and, in the 2D case, a
    different set of axes. Knowing which one a given value belongs to is
    necessary to place content where you intend it to appear.

    \section1 The three coordinate systems

    \table
    \header
        \li Coordinate system
        \li Unit
        \li Axes
        \li Origin
    \row
        \li XR tracking space
        \li Meter, converted to centimeters before any value reaches QML
        \li \c{+x} right, \c{+y} up, \c{-z} forward
        \li Selected with \l{XrView::referenceSpace}{XrView.referenceSpace}
    \row
        \li 3D scene: any \l Node inside an \l XrView
        \li Centimeter
        \li \c{+x} right, \c{+y} up, \c{-z} forward
        \li The parent node
    \row
        \li 2D content: any \l Item
        \li Pixel
        \li \c{+x} right, \c{+y} \e down
        \li The top-left corner of the parent item
    \endtable

    \image qt3dxr-coordinate-systems.webp {A Qt Quick 3D scene with red, green
           and blue arrows for the +x, +y and +z axes, and a built-in cube one
           grid square wide resting on the floor}
    \caption The middle row of the table, drawn as a real scene: the built-in
    \c{#Cube} at scale 1 is 100 units across, which is one meter and one square
    of the one-meter grid.

    That cube is the whole unit rule in one object:

    \snippet qt3dxr-coordinate-systems.qml unit cube

    The tracking space and the 3D scene share the same right-handed axis
    convention, so a pose never needs to be mirrored or swizzled. Only the unit
    differs: \qxr multiplies every position it receives from the XR runtime by
    100 before it reaches the scene. An application therefore never handles
    meters directly, and never needs to convert a tracked pose itself.

    2D content is the exception. Qt Quick's \c y axis grows downwards, which is
    why moving a 2D child of a \l Node down the screen means increasing its
    \c y, while moving the \l Node itself down means decreasing its \c y. See
    \l{Qt Quick 3D Scenes with 2D Content} for the general rules, and
    \l{Placing 2D content on a virtual surface} below for the XR specifics.

    \section1 One unit is one centimeter

    In a plain Qt Quick 3D scene the unit is arbitrary: nothing outside the
    scene constrains it, so an application is free to treat a unit as a meter,
    an inch, or anything else, as long as it is consistent.

    In \qxr the unit is fixed. Tracked content has to line up with the physical
    world, and \qxr expresses the scene in centimeters:

    \table
    \header
        \li Real-world length
        \li Scene value
    \row
        \li 1 mm
        \li 0.1
    \row
        \li 1 cm
        \li 1
    \row
        \li 1 m
        \li 100
    \endtable

    This applies to every distance in the scene, not just to positions: the
    dimensions of models, the \l{XrItem::width}{width} and
    \l{XrItem::height}{height} of an \l XrItem, light ranges, and the
    \l{XrCamera::clipNear}{clip planes} of an \l XrCamera are all in
    centimeters.

    Some values to calibrate against: a desk is about 75 high, a door about 200
    high and 90 wide, and a seated user's eyes are about 120 above the floor, a
    standing user's about 160.

    \qml
    XrView {
        referenceSpace: XrView.ReferenceSpaceLocalFloor

        Node {
            // One meter in front, at standing eye height.
            position: Qt.vector3d(0, 160, -100)

            Model {
                // #Cube is 100 units across, so this is a 20 cm cube.
                source: "#Cube"
                scale: Qt.vector3d(0.2, 0.2, 0.2)
                materials: PrincipledMaterial { baseColor: "red" }
            }
        }
    }
    \endqml

    An asset that was authored in meters therefore arrives in the scene 100
    times too small. Correct this when importing the asset rather than by
    scaling nodes in the scene, so that the values you read and write in QML
    stay in centimeters. \l{Balsam Asset Import Tool}{Balsam} has the
    \c{--globalScale} and \c{--globalScaleValue} options for this.

    \section1 Where the scene origin is

    \l{XrView::referenceSpace}{XrView.referenceSpace} determines what the point
    \c{(0, 0, 0)} corresponds to in the physical world:

    \list
        \li \c XrView.ReferenceSpaceLocal places the scene origin at the
            user's default view position, which is above the floor. To
            place content relative to the floor, use
            \c ReferenceSpaceLocalFloor or \c ReferenceSpaceStage instead.
        \li \c XrView.ReferenceSpaceLocalFloor places the scene origin at
            floor height, directly below the user's default view position.
        \li \c XrView.ReferenceSpaceStage places the scene origin at floor
            height, in the center of the area the user has set up.
    \endlist

    \image qt3dxr-reference-spaces.webp {Three views of a standing figure where
           the scene origin sits at eye height for ReferenceSpaceLocal, on the
           floor for ReferenceSpaceLocalFloor, and at the center of a marked
           play area for ReferenceSpaceStage}
    \caption The same model at \c{y: 0} in each of the three reference spaces.
    The figure is 175 cm tall, and the floor grid is 50 cm.

    Pick the reference space first, then position content, because the choice
    shifts the whole scene vertically. A model placed at \c{y: 0} rests on the
    floor in \c ReferenceSpaceLocalFloor and \c ReferenceSpaceStage, but floats
    at head height in \c ReferenceSpaceLocal.

    Not every platform honors the request. On visionOS the reference space is
    always \c ReferenceSpaceLocalFloor, so an application written for
    \c ReferenceSpaceLocal has to check the property at run time and adjust.

    \l XrOrigin marks the scene origin in the scene. To move the user through
    the virtual scene, move or rotate the \l XrOrigin. This does not change the
    user's position in the physical world.

    Scaling the \l XrOrigin scales the user's size in the virtual world: the
    tracked motion of the user's head and hands is scaled with it. This can be
    useful in VR, for example to let the user look at a scene from a giant's
    view. In AR, or whenever scene distances must match physical distances, do
    not scale the \l XrOrigin.

    \section1 Tracked nodes and the XrOrigin

    \image qt3dxr-origin-hierarchy.svg {A node tree where XrCamera,
           XrController, and XrHandModel are children of the XrOrigin, while
           scene content sits in a separate branch under the XrView}

    \l XrCamera, \l XrController, and \l XrHandModel are tracked nodes. The XR
    runtime updates their position and rotation every frame, so treat those
    properties as read-only.

    The tracked nodes must be children of the \l XrOrigin. The XR runtime
    reports their poses relative to the reference space, and the \l XrOrigin
    represents the reference space in the scene.

    The tracking data that these types expose as plain values is also relative
    to the \l XrOrigin. This includes
    \l{XrController::pokePosition}{pokePosition},
    \l{XrController::jointPositions}{jointPositions}, and the positions of
    \l{XrSpatialAnchor}{spatial anchors}. These values match scene coordinates
    only when the \l XrOrigin is at the scene root with no transform. To compare
    them with other positions in the scene, map them to scene coordinates first:

    \qml
    XrController {
        controller: XrController.RightController

        // pokePosition is relative to the XrOrigin, so map it before
        // hit-testing the scene with it.
        property vector3d scenePos:
            view.xrOrigin.mapPositionToScene(pokePosition)

        onScenePosChanged: view.processTouch(scenePos, touchId)
    }
    \endqml

    The \l{Qt Quick 3D - XR Simple Touch Example}{Touch Example} shows this in
    context.

    \l{XrCamera}{XrCamera}'s \l{XrCamera::clipNear}{clipNear} and
    \l{XrCamera::clipFar}{clipFar} are in centimeters like every other
    distance, so the useful range is roughly 1 to 10000. Unlike a
    \l PerspectiveCamera, an \l XrCamera has no field of view to set: the
    runtime supplies the projection for each eye.

    \section1 Placing 2D content on a virtual surface

    As in any Qt Quick 3D scene, you can place a plain \l Item directly under a
    \l Node. One pixel of that item is then one scene unit, so in \qxr a
    500-pixel-wide \l Rectangle is five meters wide. \l XrItem avoids this by
    letting you set the surface size in centimeters. It also registers the
    surface with its \l XrView, so \l{XrView::processTouch()}{processTouch()}
    can send touch events to the content.

    \l XrItem is the bridge between the two unit systems. Its
    \l{XrItem::width}{width} and \l{XrItem::height}{height} are in centimeters,
    while its \l{XrItem::contentItem}{contentItem} is an ordinary \l Item sized
    in pixels. \l{XrItem::pixelsPerUnit}{pixelsPerUnit} is the conversion
    factor between the two, and by default \l XrItem derives it so that the
    content fills the surface:

    \qml
    XrItem {
        // A 50 cm by 30 cm surface.
        width: 50
        height: 30
        contentItem: Rectangle {
            width: 500
            height: 300
            color: "white"
        }
    }
    \endqml

    \image qt3dxr-xritem-units.webp {A 50 by 30 centimeter virtual surface
           holding a 500 by 300 pixel Qt Quick content item, with the node
           origin marked at its top-left corner}
    \caption With \l{XrItem::pixelsPerUnit}{pixelsPerUnit} at 10, one
    centimeter of surface holds ten pixels of content.

    With \l{XrItem::pixelsPerUnit}{pixelsPerUnit} at 10, \l XrItem scales the
    content item to a tenth of its pixel size. \l XrItem scales the rendered
    content item and does not render it at a higher resolution, so text on a
    surface much larger than its content item looks coarse. To keep text sharp,
    set \l{XrItem::manualPixelsPerUnit}{manualPixelsPerUnit} to \c true, set
    \l{XrItem::pixelsPerUnit}{pixelsPerUnit}, and size the content item to match
    the surface.

    An \l XrItem's surface extends to the right of and \e below its own origin,
    following the 2D convention. Offset it to center the surface on the node
    position:

    \qml
    XrItem {
        width: 50
        height: 30
        x: -width / 2
        y: height / 2
    }
    \endqml

    \section1 Related information

    \list
        \li \l{Qt Quick 3D Scenes with 2D Content}
        \li \l{Locomotion in \qxr}
        \li \l{\qxr QML Types}
    \endlist
*/
